[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Strategy Pattern vs. State Pattern: A Technical Deep Dive"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While the Strategy Pattern and the State Pattern share remarkably similar structural diagrams in UML, they solve fundamentally different problems in software architecture. Understanding the distinction between behavioral configuration and lifecycle management is crucial for writing maintainable object-oriented systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Structural Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At a glance, both patterns rely on composition and delegation. A Context object holds a reference to an interface (Strategy or State) and delegates execution to it. However, the intent and mutability of these references differ vastly."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"The Strategy Pattern"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Strategy Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that enables an algorithm's behavior to be selected at runtime. It defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. The strategy is typically chosen by the client and remains relatively static throughout a specific task."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public interface PaymentStrategy {
    void pay(int amount);
}

public class CreditCardStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(int amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " using Credit Card.");
    }
}

public class ShoppingCart {
    private PaymentStrategy paymentStrategy;

    public void setPaymentStrategy(PaymentStrategy strategy) {
        this.paymentStrategy = strategy;
    }

    public void checkout(int amount) {
        paymentStrategy.pay(amount);
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The State Pattern"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The State Pattern allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. The object will appear to change its class. Unlike Strategy, the state transitions are often managed internally by the Context or the States themselves, reflecting a finite state machine."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public interface OrderState {
    void next(Order order);
    void prev(Order order);
    void printStatus();
}

public class OrderedState implements OrderState {
    public void next(Order order) {
        order.setState(new DeliveredState());
    }
    public void prev(Order order) {
        System.out.println("The order is in its root state.");
    }
    public void printStatus() {
        System.out.println("Order placed, not yet delivered.");
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Intent: Strategy focuses on HOW to achieve a goal (algorithm interchangeability). State focuses on WHEN behavior changes based on internal state transitions."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Knowledge: In Strategy, concrete strategies typically do not know about other strategies or the context details. In State, states often know about adjacent states and frequently trigger transitions themselves."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mutability: Strategy is usually injected by the client and can remain unchanged. State frequently mutates over the lifetime of the Context object as operations are performed."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose the Strategy Pattern when you have multiple ways to perform a specific task and want to swap algorithms dynamically. Choose the State Pattern when an object's behavior depends heavily on its current state and must change its behavior dynamically at runtime based on workflow transitions."}]
